/// A serial line: on a USB converter (`usb_serial`, 800-LNK-0002), the FTDI FT232R and the WCH CH340, which the
/// maintainer's RS-485 converters carry; or on a board's UART (`uart_serial`, 1.1, 100000-LNK-0038), a 16550, a PL011
/// or the kernel's console channel. `init` lends it to one client, the link service (800-LNK-0003, MC-2.3).
/// Bytes go as they are, 8 data bits, no parity, one stop bit, no flow control: the protocol above keeps a half-duplex
/// line to one sender. The driver reads the line all the time and keeps what came until the client takes it.
package mind:serial@1.1.0;

interface serial {
    /// `no-device`: no converter is attached, or it went; `invalid`: a rate the chip cannot make within 3 % (on a UART
    /// whose clock is the board's, any rate but the one the firmware left it at), or an empty write; `failed`: the
    /// converter refused a request or did not answer, or the kernel's console channel ended.
    enum error { no-device, invalid, failed }

    /// The line: its chip (1 FTDI FT232R, 2 WCH CH340; 3 a 16550 at ports, 4 a PL011, 5 the kernel's console channel,
    /// since 1.1), its vendor and product IDs (0 on a UART), the rate asked for and the rate the chip makes of it
    /// (bits/s), and the bytes lost because the client did not take them in time.
    record info { chip: u8, vendor: u16, product: u16, baud: u32, actual: u32, lost: u32 }

    info: func() -> result<info, error>;
    /// Sets the rate; answers the rate the chip makes of it.
    configure: func(baud: u32) -> result<u32, error>;
    /// Sends `data`, answering when the converter (or the UART, or the kernel's queue) has taken all of it.
    write: func(data: bytes<4096>) -> result<_, error>;
    /// The bytes received since the last call, oldest first, up to 4096; none when nothing came.
    read: func() -> result<bytes<4096>, error>;
    /// Answers when bytes are waiting, or after `timeout-ms` (at most 1000) with none; returns how many are.
    wait: func(timeout-ms: u32) -> u32;
}
